To manage a Jenkins pipeline to use a specific slave instead of the master, you can use the agent directive in your pipeline script to specify the label of the desired slave node.
Here's an example:
pipeline {
    agent {
        label 'my-slave-label'
    }
    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                // your build steps here
            }
        }
        // add more stages as needed
    }
}
In this example, the agent directive specifies that the pipeline should run on a node with the label my-slave-label. Jenkins will automatically schedule the pipeline on a slave node that has this label assigned.
You can also use the node directive to achieve the same result:
pipeline {
    options {
        buildDiscarder(logRotator(numToKeepStr: '10'))
    }
    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            node('my-slave-label') {
                // your build steps here
            }
        }
        // add more stages as needed
    }
}
In this example, the node directive specifies that the Build stage should run on a node with the label my-slave-label. The options directive specifies that Jenkins should keep only the last 10 builds for this pipeline.

